^^Imperative^^
Imperative is the form of a verb that you shout to someone, ‘go there!’ or ‘don’t do it!’. This is done by taking the I-form of the verb (the first person singular) and removing the last 'n':

mennä, menen --> mene! (go!)
juosta, juoksen --> juokse! (run!)
saada, saan --> saa! (get!)

In Finnish there are different forms for imperative depending on if you say it to one person or many people, e.g. *mene!* (go! singular) and *menkää!* (go! plural). For the plural, usually you remove the two last letters *of the nominative* instead of the last 'n' from the first person singular, and then add *-kaa/-kää*:

mennä --> men*kää*!
juosta --> juos*kaa*!
saada --> saa*kaa*!

^Additional notes on pronunciation^
As already mentioned, in Finnish it’s important to pronounce every syllable. One quirk of pronounciation that you will come into contact with in almost all spoken sentences is Gemination, and it occurs mainly in imperative phrases followingly:

Mene sinne! (Go there!) is pronounced *menes-sinne* (long 's')
Ole hiljaa! (Be quiet!) is pronounced *oleh-hiljaa* (long 'h')
Muista pestä peppusi! (Remember to wash your butt!) is pronounced *muistap-pestäp-peppusi*

What happens here is essentially the same thing as in the word *matto* from the introduction, where there is a noticeable pause between the ‘a’ and ‘t’ sound, except here it occurs between words and you pronounce the next word’s first letter in advance.
You have also heard this in Unit 0 in the word *tervetuloa* pronounced as ‘tervettuloa’ with two ‘t’. This is because it’s actually a compound word from *terve* (healthy) and *tulo* (coming) + *-a* (partitive). The word *terve* ends in an ‘e’, and all words ending in ‘e’ get the next sound doubled, regardless of if it is in the beginning of the next separate word (menes-sinne!) or if it happens inside a compound word (tervet-tuloa)
This is a small detail and it is not always very apparent, but it is good to know when learning the imperative. Please pay extra attention to the pronounciation in this Unit, as this same thing will be important when learning to say ‘I don’t do something’.